### What is the problem?

Given a string, find the length of the longest contiguous substring that contains no repeated character. This is the same problem as Q1445 but solved with a HashMap optimization that avoids the inner while loop for removing characters from the window.

```java
package com.bank.dsa;

import java.util.HashMap;
import java.util.Map;

public class LongestUniqueHashMap {
    public static int lengthOfLongestSubstring(String s) {
        Map<Character, Integer> lastSeen = new HashMap<>();
        int left = 0, best = 0;
        for (int right = 0; right < s.length(); right++) {
            char c = s.charAt(right);
            if (lastSeen.containsKey(c) && lastSeen.get(c) >= left) {
                left = lastSeen.get(c) + 1;
            }
            lastSeen.put(c, right);
            best = Math.max(best, right - left + 1);
        }
        return best;
    }
}
```

### Why use HashMap instead of HashSet?

The HashSet version uses an inner while loop to remove characters from the left until the duplicate is gone. The HashMap version stores the index of each character's last occurrence, allowing the left pointer to jump directly to one past that index in a single operation. Both are O(n) amortized, but the HashMap version does fewer operations per step.

```java
package com.bank.dsa;

public class SetVsMapDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String s = "ABCBDA";

        // HashSet version: inner while loop removes A, B before adding B again
        // HashMap version: jumps left directly to index 2 (one past previous B)

        System.out.println("HashSet: " + LongestUniqueSubstring.lengthOfLongestSubstring(s));
        System.out.println("HashMap: " + LongestUniqueHashMap.lengthOfLongestSubstring(s));
    }
}
```

```text
HashSet: 4
HashMap: 4
```

### Why is the at-least-left guard critical?

Without the guard `lastSeen.get(c) >= left`, a character seen before the current window would incorrectly pull the left pointer backward. For example, in "ABBA", after processing the second B, left moves to index 2. When processing the second A, the stored index for A is 0, which is before left. Jumping to index 1 would be moving backward, corrupting the window.

```java
package com.bank.dsa;

import java.util.HashMap;
import java.util.Map;

public class GuardDemo {
    // WRONG: no >= left guard
    public static int buggy(String s) {
        Map<Character, Integer> last = new HashMap<>();
        int left = 0, best = 0;
        for (int right = 0; right < s.length(); right++) {
            char c = s.charAt(right);
            if (last.containsKey(c)) {
                left = last.get(c) + 1; // BUG: may move left backward!
            }
            last.put(c, right);
            best = Math.max(best, right - left + 1);
        }
        return best;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Buggy ABBA:   " + buggy("ABBA"));     // 3 (wrong!)
        System.out.println("Correct ABBA: " + LongestUniqueHashMap.lengthOfLongestSubstring("ABBA")); // 2
    }
}
```

```text
Buggy ABBA:   3
Correct ABBA: 2
```

### How does the algorithm trace through ABBA?

Walking through "ABBA" step by step shows why the guard matters:

```java
package com.bank.dsa;

import java.util.HashMap;
import java.util.Map;

public class TraceDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String s = "ABBA";
        Map<Character, Integer> last = new HashMap<>();
        int left = 0, best = 0;
        for (int right = 0; right < s.length(); right++) {
            char c = s.charAt(right);
            if (last.containsKey(c) && last.get(c) >= left) {
                left = last.get(c) + 1;
            }
            last.put(c, right);
            best = Math.max(best, right - left + 1);
            System.out.println("right=" + right + " c=" + c + " left=" + left + " best=" + best + " last=" + last);
        }
    }
}
```

```text
right=0 c=A left=0 best=1 last={A=0}
right=1 c=B left=0 best=2 last={A=0, B=1}
right=2 c=B left=2 best=2 last={A=0, B=2}
right=3 c=A left=2 best=2 last={A=3, B=2}
```

### How do you use an array instead of HashMap?

For ASCII characters, replace the HashMap with an int array of size 128 initialized to -1. The character's ASCII value serves as the index. This avoids hashing overhead and gives constant-time lookups with fixed memory.

```java
package com.bank.dsa;

import java.util.Arrays;

public class ArrayOptimized {
    public static int lengthOfLongestSubstring(String s) {
        int[] lastSeen = new int[128];
        Arrays.fill(lastSeen, -1);
        int left = 0, best = 0;
        for (int right = 0; right < s.length(); right++) {
            char c = s.charAt(right);
            if (lastSeen[c] >= left) {
                left = lastSeen[c] + 1;
            }
            lastSeen[c] = right;
            best = Math.max(best, right - left + 1);
        }
        return best;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(lengthOfLongestSubstring("HDFC2001ABCD")); // 9
    }
}
```

```text
9
```

### What are the edge cases?

An empty string returns 0. A single character returns 1. All identical characters return 1. All unique characters return the full length. The at-least-left guard correctly handles all these cases.

```java
package com.bank.dsa;

public class EdgeCaseDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(LongestUniqueHashMap.lengthOfLongestSubstring(""));      // 0
        System.out.println(LongestUniqueHashMap.lengthOfLongestSubstring("A"));     // 1
        System.out.println(LongestUniqueHashMap.lengthOfLongestSubstring("AAAA"));  // 1
        System.out.println(LongestUniqueHashMap.lengthOfLongestSubstring("ABCD"));  // 4
    }
}
```

```text
0
1
1
4
```

### What is the time and space complexity?

Time is O(n) because the right pointer visits each character once and the left pointer only moves forward. There is no inner loop. Space is O(min(n, alphabet size)) for the HashMap, or O(1) with the fixed-size array for ASCII.

